r the  rest of his life  he beats up the school bullies that  routinely harass his adopted sister  lenora  and then executes preston t garden the  twisted preacher whose abuse leads to  her death  killing teagarden sets off a chain  reaction of violence as arvin flees the  scene of the crime only to be picked up  by serial killers carl and sandy  arvin kills the couple in self-defense  but is pursued by sandy's brother  corrupt sheriff lee beaudeker who is  determined to cover up his sister's  crimes and safeguard his reputation  arvind winds up killing beaudecker as  well and ultimately hitches a new ride  to cincinnati  as he rides away he considers one day  going back home and making amends with  his grandmother  starting a family of his own or  enlisting to fight in the vietnam war  we don't know which of these paths arvin  ultimately takes or if he'll be able to  break the cycle of violence that's  followed him his whole life  at the end of the devil all the time  libo decker's determination to hunt down  and kill arvin doesn't feel motivated by  brotherly grief over the killing of his  sister but by fear  what is beau decker so afraid of well  pretty much everything  bodaker has been chasing money and power  since the first time we met him and  spending his time as sheriff taking  bribes and cutting deals  in the final act of the film bodecker is  running for re-election and is terrified  that the skeletons in his closet could  come spilling out  at first bodecker is just afraid that  his political opponent will discover  that he's been taking bribes and  greasing the wheels for local corrupt  business owners  or that he's been looking the other way  while his sister engages in illegal sex  work  before arvin ever crosses his path he's  already committed two murders to try to  cover up his tracks  but the deaths of sandy and carl lead  beaudeker to uncover their secret stash  of photographs of their victims  rather than do his duty and report what  he's found vodecker destroys the  evidence terrified that if sandy's  activities come to light it will cost  him the election and all the money and  power that comes with it  arvind remains the one loose end a  witness to sandy and carl's crimes  even though arvin has no intention of  sticking around bodecker is determined  to make sure he can never reveal what he  knows about sandy  unfortunately for him arvin is quicker  on the draw and bodecker's paranoia and  lust for power winds up being his  downfall  we get a lot of clues throughout the  devil all the time that sandy is  considering leaving her husband and  abandoning their life of murder and  secrecy  she gets as far as loading up her car  and preparing to drive off but has  second thoughts and returns home  later right before she's killed she even  considers killing carl and driving off  with arvin  but it turns out that carl had grown  distrustful of sandy going so far as to  replace the bullets in her gun with  blanks which is how arvin's life is  spared and sandy's is lost  but would sandy actually have left had  she remained alive we'll never know but  several signs point to no  first of course is the fact that she  didn't leave when she had the chance  of course that doesn't mean she never  would have gone through with it but the  fact that she never takes any of the  opportunities presented to her seemed to  indicate that while she liked the idea  of leaving she wasn't necessarily  determined to actually do it  but the biggest indicator that she would  have stayed is that she actually enjoys  what she and carl do together  before sheriff bodecker visits her house  we see her looking fondly at the photos  of her and carl's victims showing that  she's not just going along with the  murders for his sake she actually gets  pleasure from it herself  as tired as she is of carl she doesn't  seem to be able to give up the killing  which makes her marriage to him a  necessary evil  as arvin hitchhikes away from knock em  stiff at the end of the film a snippet  of an address from president johnson  plays on the radio  justifying his decision to send more  troops to vietnam  that we cannot be defeated by force of  arms  or by superior power while one could  dismiss the speech as background chatter  the quote that made its way into the  film feels significant in a variety of  ways  after all arvin's story began with  another war with his father returning  from world war ii haunted by his  memories of what he experienced  willard's background as a soldier  largely informed the way he parented  arvin  and how he taught him how to solve his  problems with violence then there's the  choice johnson is making to commit more  troops to fighting the war and his  declaration that the united states won't  be strong-armed into backing down  this echoes willard's philosophy about  how to deal with aggressors  them boys might be bigger than you but  the next time one of them starts his  i want you to finish it it's a belief  arvin carries with him long after his  father  is gone which is what drives him to  attack lenora's bullies and the preacher  who abused her  yet at the end of the film as johnson  speaks arvin gives a tremendous yawn and  begins to drift off and dream of a  better life  perhaps this symbolizes that he's  managed to let go of this violent  philosophy  at last and that it no longer holds  power over him  in his director's statement antonio  campos says that one of the things that  drew him to adapt the devil all the time  is its exploration of generational faith  and the elements of religion that get  passed down from parent to child the  director explains that he was raised by  a devout mother and a disillusioned  father  and that rather than having his film  serve as an indictment of faith in  religion he views this story as a way to  explore it  campos writes like the characters in the  story i've inherited a little bit of  both my parents extremes and over the  last several years i've tried to  understand these two extremes within me  i still struggle with my faith and  continue to question it and as a  filmmaker i use storytelling to find  answers  that struggle is clear in the devil all  the time with many of the film's  characters motivated or manipulated  according to their strong religious  convictions  it doesn't shy away from the many ways  in which religion can be twisted to  cause harm  but it also leaves the door open for the  existence of god and some sort of larger  divine plan  speaking to esquire campos said the film  plays with two ideas  one that things are random because  there's the absence of god throughout  the movie but also the way that  everything plays out suggests there is a  bigger force at play  the movie is trying to explore human  beings on earth struggling to connect  and see god  showing there is some greater design but  they can't see it part of this greater  design could be the surprising ways in  which the characters lives intersect for  example serial killers carl and sandy  meet on the same day in the very same  diner where arvid's parents meet  carl and sandy go on to kill lenora's  father leading her to live with arvin's  grandmother which is how arvin and  lenora wind up his siblings  arvin ultimately kills carl and sandy  after avenging lenora none of them even  realize how their fates all trace back  to the same day in the diner all those  years ago  running parallel to the themes of faith  and religious fanaticism and the devil  all the time is the theme of how giving  into violence always begets more  violence  according to campos the devil all the  time is a generational story about the  cyclical pattern of violence  the story is very simply about what we  inherit and what we pass on  this pattern becomes extremely apparent  in arvin who follows closely in his  father's footsteps and how he deals with  threats to his family  even when arvin decides to walk away  from his life after killing the preacher  violence follows him and three more  people are dead by the end of the film  people who are all brought into arvind's  orbit due to violent cycles of their own  it's no coincidence that the narrative  of the film is sandwiched between two  wars  both of which left deep scars on history  at the end of the film with arvin  dreaming of either starting a new life  or enlisting in the war the film seems  to imply that there may be no escaping  this brutal cycle  but that it's worth trying anyway  overall the devil all the time is a  pretty bleak story with a vast majority  of its characters meeting rather  miserable and bloody ends  but against all odds arvin manages to  survive and head off to seek a fresh  start  although we'll never know if he's  actually successful at the ending of the  film campo said  things just have a way of sorting  themselves out and knock them stiff and  even if you don't get a happy ending  there's still the potential for a better  beginning  this seems to speak directly to arvin's  story which culminates in a series of  unlikely encounters which appeared a nod  to divine intervention  maybe it's the very improbability of  arvin's ending that speaks to hope for a  better future  after all the odds of arvin crossing  paths with the same people who met in  the same room as his parents and killed  his sister's father don't seem very high  nor to the odds of him surviving that  encounter perhaps the point of arvind's  story is to show that sometimes  people can beat the odds and break out  of harmful cycles no matter how deeply  entrenched they are  in that sense maybe the ending of the  film isn't quite so bleak after all  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite  movies and tv shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

